  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 955 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAnother quiet night with clear skies and winds out of the south.\nQuiet pattern continues through the next several days as the LLJ\ncontinues to stream in dry air from west Texas/New Mexico. A\nmid-level trough moving through tonight won't do much more than\nslightly shift winds to southwesterly Tuesday morning. We'll see\nwarmer air build in behind this mid-level wave bringing\ntemperatures back up into the low 80s Tuesday afternoon. Expect\nonly one day of this warmer weather though as a dry cold front\nwill push through Tuesday night into early Wednesday bringing\nback northerly winds and milder temperatures. Highs on Tuesday\npeak in the mid-to-upper 70s. \n\nA northwesterly flow pattern sets in for the latter half of the\nweek keeping temperatures milder with highs in the low-to-mid\n70s for Thursday and Friday. Shortwaves may bring occasional\nhigh-level clouds but dry lower-levels will keep precip chances\nlow. Next low (20-30%) chance for showers/storms is Thursday\nnight into early Friday when a warm front may bring in some \nlower-level moisture but may not be a very noticeable amount.\nDry air could keep much from reaching the ground. \n\nThe ridge over the west coast finally moves through over the\nreestablishing dry conditions and warmer temperatures. A wetter\npattern looks to set back up next week, corroborated by the \nCPC's 6-10 day outlook.",
